8:   1. (1)\nL579: \nL580: There is an inclusion $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL581: \nL582:   2. (2)\nL583: \nL584: For every $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$ admitting a surjection $Z\\twoheadrightarrow X$.\nL604: by cite86†Theorem 5.1 , so in particular, cite129†1 holds. It follows from cite96†Theorem 4.6 cite130†2 that\nL605: \nL606:  | $${\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))}.$$  |\nL607: \nL608: Finally, because $\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$, cite131†2 must hold by cite120†Definition 4.4 .\nL610:  | $$\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})).$$  |\nL611: \nL612: We conclude that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$ by cite86†Theorem 5.1 . ∎\nL613: \nL614: We now characterize the 1-pop-stack sortable elements of $\\tors\\Lambda$.\nL615: ###### Corollary 5.4.\nL616: \nL617: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$. The following are equivalent.\nL618: \nL619:   1. (1)\nL620: \nL621: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\hat{0}$.\nL622: \nL623:   2. (2)\nL624: \nL625: Every brick in $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$ is simple.\nL626: \nL627:   3. (3)\nL628: \nL629: The torsion class $\\mathcal{T}$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL630: \nL631:   4. (4)\nL632: \nL633: There exists a nonzero injective module $I$ such that $\\mathcal{T}=\\prescript{\\perp}{}{I}$.\nL634: \nL635: Dually, the following are equivalent.\nL636: \nL637:   1. (1)\nL638: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL639: \nL640:   2. (2)\nL641: \nL642: Every brick in $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$ is simple.\nL643: \nL644:   3. (3)\nL645: \nL646: The torsion-free class $\\mathcal{T}^{\\perp}$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL647: \nL648:   4. (4)\nL649: \nL650: There exists a nonzero projective module $P$ such that $\\mathcal{T}=\\Gen(P)$.\nL651: ###### Proof.\nL652: \nL653: We prove only the first result as the second is dual. The equivalences among cite132†2 , cite133†3 , and cite134†4 are contained in cite135†Proposition 3.10 .\nL654: \nL655: To see that cite136†1 implies cite132†2 , suppose $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\hat{0}$. Then every brick in $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))$ is simple. But $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))$ by cite137†Theorem 5.3 .\nL656: We now show that cite133†3 implies cite136†1 . Suppose $\\mathcal{T}$ is a Serre subcategory. Then in particular, $\\mathcal{T}$ is closed under submodules. Thus, for $Y\\in\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$, the map $g_{Y,\\mathcal{X}}$ must be surjective. cite86†Theorem 5.1 then implies that $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))=\\emptyset$, so $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=0$. ∎\nL657: The following characterizes all 2-pop-stack sortable elements of $\\tors\\Lambda$; it also describes the image under $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ of each 2-pop-stack sortable element.\nL658: ###### Corollary 5.5.\nL659: \nL660: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$, and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be a set of simple modules. Then $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ if and only if the following all hold.\nL661: \nL662:   1. (1)\nL663: \nL664: We have $\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))=0=\\mathrm{Ext}^{1}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$.\nL665: \nL666:   2. (2)\nL667: The socle^{2}^{2} 2 Recall that the socle $\\mathrm{soc}M$ of a module $M$ is the sum of all of its semisimple submodules. $\\mathrm{soc}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ of $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$ satisfies $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})/\\mathrm{soc}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$.\nL668: \nL669:   3. (3)\nL670: \nL671: There does not exist $S\\in\\mathcal{S}$ with $\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),S)=0$. (Equivalently, there is a surjection $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\twoheadrightarrow\\mathcal{S}$.)\nL672: ###### Proof.\nL673: Suppose first that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$. Since $\\mathcal{D}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))=\\mathcal{S}$, it follows from cite137†Theorem 5.3 that $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$. Then the fact that cite138†1 holds follows from the definition of a 2-term simple-minded collection.\nL674: cite137†Theorem 5.3 also implies that for every $S\\in\\mathcal{S}$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ that admits a surjection $Z\\twoheadrightarrow S$. This shows that cite139†3 holds. To prove cite140†2 , consider ${X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))}$. Since $\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ is closed under submodules, the map $g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\colon X\\rightarrow S_{X}$ must be surjective.\nL675: Moreover, we have that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))=0$ by cite87†Corollary 5.4 . cite86†Theorem 5.1 implies that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ is in $\\mathcal{U}(0)$, which is precisely the set of simple modules. If follows that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\subseteq\\mathrm{soc}(X)$ and therefore that $X/\\mathrm{soc}(X)\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$.\nL676: To prove the converse, suppose $\\mathcal{T}$ satisfies cite138†Items 1 , cite140†2 and cite139†3 . Then cite138†1 implies that $(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ is a semibrick pair. Moreover, for $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, the map $g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ must be surjective since $\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ is closed under submodules. This map must also have a semisimple kernel by cite140†2 .\nL677: We then have that $\\mu_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}\\cup\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime})$ for $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}=\\{\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\mid X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\}$. cite141†Proposition A.3 therefore implies that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ is simple for all $X$.\nL678: Letting $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime}$ denote the set of all simple modules, we find that $(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime})$ is a 2-term simple-minded collection satisfying $\\mathcal{S}\\cup\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}\\subseteq\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime}$. By cite142†Proposition A.4 , $(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{Y}):=\\mu_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime})$ is a 2-term simple-minded collection satisfying $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{Y}$.\nL679: Now note that $\\mathcal{Y}=\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$ by cite112†Theorem 3.7 . Hence, $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$. Together with cite139†3 , this allows us to use cite137†Theorem 5.3 to conclude that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$. ∎\nL680: ### 5.3. The image of pop-stack\nL681: \nL682: We now build toward a complete description of the images of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ and $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ (and, hence, the facets of the canonical join complex of $\\tors\\Lambda$) for any $\\tau$-tilting finite algebra $\\Lambda$. Our first result is proven more generally for arbitrary semidistrim lattices in [cite59†DW23 , Section 9]. We give a new representation-theoretic proof for lattices of torsion classes.\nL683: ###### Proposition 5.6.\nL684: \nL685: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$.\nL686: \nL687:   1. (1)\nL688: \nL689: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\subseteq\\mathcal{T}$.\nL690: \nL691:   2. (2)\nL692: \nL693: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\supseteq\\mathcal{T}$.\nL694: \nL695:   3. (3)\nL696: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL697: \nL698:   4. (4)\nL699: \nL700: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL701: ###### Proof.\nL702: \nL703: We prove only cite143†1 and cite144†3 as the proofs of cite145†2 and cite146†4 are similar.\nL704: \nL705: We have $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))$ by cite86†Theorem 5.1 . By cite110†Theorem 3.4 , this implies cite143†1 .\nL706: To prove cite144†3 , let $(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))$. Let $(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})=\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})$ and $(\\mathcal{X}_{2},\\mathcal{Y}_{2})=\\mu_{\\uparrow}(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})$. By cite86†Theorem 5.1 , we need to show that $\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X}_{2},\\mathcal{Y}_{2})=(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})$.\nL707: By cite120†Definition 4.4 , this is equivalent to showing that $\\mathcal{X}_{2}=\\mathcal{Y}_{1}$, which is equivalent to showing that $g_{\\mathcal{Y}_{1},Z}$ is surjective for every $Z\\in\\mathcal{X}_{1}$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPop-Stack Operators for Torsion Classes and Cambrian Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1)\nciteturn9538view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9537view1\",\"pattern\":\"9.\"}); Total lines: 1931\nL1138: ###### Remark 7.9.\nL1375: ###### Remark 9.2.\nL1460: Let $\\mathrm{H}_{c}=\\mathrm{Heap}(\\mathsf{sort}_{\\mathsf{c}}(w_{\\circ}))$ and $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{H}}_{c}=\\mathrm{Heap}(\\psi(\\mathsf{sort}_{\\mathsf{c}}(w_{\\circ})))$; note that $\\mathrm{H}_{c}$ and $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{H}}_{c}$ depend only on the Coxeter element $c$ (and not the reduced word $\\mathsf{c}$). It follows from [cite227†STW , Lemma 2.6.5] that\nL1461: \nL1462: (9.1)  |  | $$\\mathsf{sort}_{\\mathsf{c}}(w_{\\circ})\\psi(\\mathsf{sort}_{\\mathsf{c}}(w_{\\circ}))\\equiv\\mathsf{c}^{h}.$$  |\nL1463: Therefore, we can think of $\\mathrm{H}_{c}$ as an order ideal of $\\mathrm{Heap}(\\mathsf{c}^{h})$ whose complement (in $\\mathrm{Heap}(\\mathsf{c}^{h})$) is $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{H}}_{c}$. Define\nL1464: \nL1465:  | $$\\mathbf{z}_{c}=\\zeta(\\mathrm{H}_{c}^{\\leq h-1}).$$  |\nL1466: ###### Remark 9.9.\nL1467: We have defined $\\mathbf{z}_{c}$ using Coxeter–Catalan combinatorics because we deemed this approach to be the most amenable for proving that $|\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}(\\mathbf{z}_{c})|=h$. However, there is also a more direct lattice-theoretic way to define $\\mathbf{z}_{c}$. The spine of $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$, denoted $\\mathrm{spine}(\\mathrm{Camb}_{c})$, is the union of the maximum-length chains of $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$.\nL1468: Thomas and Williams [cite70†TW19b ] proved that $\\mathrm{spine}(\\mathrm{Camb}_{c})$ is a distributive sublattice of $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$. It is also known [cite70†TW19b ] that\nL1469:  | $$\\mathrm{spine}(\\mathrm{Camb}_{c})=\\{\\zeta(I)\\mid I\\in\\mathcal{J}(\\mathrm{H}_{c})\\}.$$  |\nL1470: \nL1471: One can show that $\\mathbf{z}_{c}=(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\mathrm{spine}(\\mathrm{Camb}_{c})})^{h-1}(e)$ (where $e=\\hat{0}$ is the identity element).\nL1472: ###### Example 9.10.\nL1473: \nL1474: Suppose $W=A_{8}$. The edges in $\\Gamma_{W}$ are $\\{s_{i},s_{i+1}\\}$ for $1\\leq i\\leq 7$. The Coxeter number of $W$ is $h=9$. The map $\\psi$ is given by $\\psi(s_{k})=s_{9-k}$. Let $c=s_{1}s_{3}s_{2}s_{4}s_{6}s_{5}s_{7}s_{8}$ and $\\mathsf{c}=\\mathsf{s}_{1}\\mathsf{s}_{3}\\mathsf{s}_{2}\\mathsf{s}_{4}\\mathsf{s}_{6}\\mathsf{s}_{5}\\mathsf{s}_{7}\\mathsf{s}_{8}$. The acyclic orientation of $\\Gamma_{W}$ corresponding to $c$ is\nL1476: cite229†Figure 6 shows the Hasse diagram of $\\mathrm{Heap}(\\mathsf{c}^{9})$; it is drawn sideways so that each cover relation $\\mathsf{s}_{i}^{(j)}\\lessdot\\mathsf{s}_{i^{\\prime}}^{(j^{\\prime})}$ is drawn with $\\mathsf{s}_{i}^{(j)}$ to the left of $\\mathsf{s}_{i^{\\prime}}^{(j^{\\prime})}$. As explained in cite38†Section 9.4 , this Hasse diagram is referred to as the combinatorial AR quiver of $\\mathsf{c}^{9}$ in [cite227†STW , Section 9.2].\nL1512: ###### Example 9.12.\nL1536: ###### Example 9.14.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Pop-Stack Operator on Ornamentation Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2501.10311v1)\nciteturn9538view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9537view2\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 1.1\"}); Total lines: 456\nL81: ###### Theorem 1.1.\nL82: \nL83: Let $\\mathsf{T}\\in\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$, and let $\\mathfrak{r}$ be the root of $\\mathsf{T}$. Then\nL84: \nL85:  | $$\\max_{\\delta\\in\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})}|\\text{$\\mathchar 525$}_{\\mathsf{Pop}}(\\delta)|=\\max_{C\\in\\mathcal{M}_{\\mathsf{T}}}\\min_{v\\in C\\setminus\\{{\\mathfrak{r}}\\}}(|\\Delta_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)|+2\\,0pt_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)-1).$$  |\nL86: Let $\\mathsf{C}_{n}$ denote the chain with $n$ nodes. The ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n})$ is the $n$-th Tamari lattice. In this special case, cite41†Theorem 1.1 tells us that the maximum size of a forward orbit of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on the $n$-th Tamari lattice is $n$, which agrees with the aforementioned result from [cite36†10 ].\nL87: \nL88: The next theorem characterizes the image of the pop-stack operator on an ornamentation lattice, thereby generalizing a result of Hong from [cite38†14 ].\nL89: ###### Theorem 1.2.\nL90: \nL91: Let $\\mathsf{T}\\in\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$ and $\\delta\\in\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$. For each $u\\in\\mathsf{T}$, let $o_{u}$ be the minimal ornament of $\\delta$ that properly contains $\\delta(u)$; if no such ornament exists, let $o_{u}=\\mathsf{T}$. Then $\\delta\\in\\mathsf{Pop}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T}))$ if and only if for every $u\\in\\mathsf{T}$ and every child $u^{\\prime}$ of $u$ in $\\delta(u)$, we have $\\Delta_{\\delta(u)}(u^{\\prime})\\neq\\Delta_{o_{u}}(u^{\\prime})$.\nL92: In cite17†Section 6 , we will prove that every ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ is in fact semdistributive. This means that $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ has a canonical join complex, the number of facets of which is $|\\mathsf{Pop}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T}))|$. This provides an alternative interpretation of cite42†Theorem 1.2 . See [cite37†12 , Corollary 9.10] for other interpretations of the size of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on a semidistributive lattice.\nL93: It is also natural to consider the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ when $k\\geq 2$. So far, this has not been explored in the literature, largely because it is often difficult to characterize this image. We will prove some necessary conditions for an ornamentation to be in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ (see cite43†Propositions 3.3 and cite44†5.3 ). These conditions are, in general, not sufficient.\nL94: However, we will obtain as a consequence a complete characterization of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n}))$, where $\\mathsf{C}_{n}$ is an $n$-element chain poset (see cite45†Theorem 5.4 ). This will allow us to deduce the following explicit generating function for the sizes of the images of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ on Tamari lattices.\nL95: ###### Theorem 1.3.\nL96: \nL97: For each nonnegative integer $k$, we have\nL98: \nL99:  | $$\\sum_{n\\geq 0}|\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n}))|x^{n}=\\frac{1-x^{k+1}-\\sqrt{(1-x^{k+1})^{2}-4x(1-x)(1-x^{k+1})}}{2x(1-x)}.$$  |\nL100: When $k=0$, the generating function in cite46†Theorem 1.3 is that of the Catalan numbers, which we already knew since $\\mathsf{Pop}^{0}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n}))=\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n})$ is the $n$-th Tamari lattice. When $k=1$, the generating function is that of the Motzkin numbers; this recovers one of Hong’s main results from [cite38†14 ].\nL101: ### 1.4. Outline\nL102: cite11†Section 2 discusses basic properties of the pop-stack operator on an ornamentation lattice and introduces some notation and terminology. We prove cite41†Theorem 1.1 in cite12†Section 3 after establishing a necessary condition for an ornamentation to be in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ (cite43†Proposition 3.3 ). We prove cite42†Theorem 1.2 in cite15†Section 4 . In cite16†Section 5 , we prove cite44†Proposition 5.3 , which yields another constraint on the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$.\nL103: We then characterize the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ on the $n$-th Tamari lattice in cite45†Theorem 5.4 , and we deduce cite46†Theorem 1.3 . Finally, cite17†Section 6 provides a short proof that ornamentation lattices are semidistributive.\nL104: ## 2. Basics\nL105: Let $\\mathsf{T}\\in\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$ be a rooted plane tree with root node $\\mathfrak{r}$. All of our main results are trivial when $n=1$, so we will tacitly assume that $n\\geq 2$. The length of a chain with $\\ell$ elements is $\\ell-1$. For $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$, let $0pt_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)$ and $0pt_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)$ denote the height and depth of $v$ in $\\mathsf{T}$.\nL106: That is, $0pt_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)$ is the maximum length of a chain in $\\Delta_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)$, and $0pt_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)$ is the length of the chain $\\nabla_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)$. A descendant of $v$ is an element of $\\Delta_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)\\setminus\\{v\\}$, and a child of $v$ is an element covered by $v$. Let $\\mathrm{ch}_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)$ be the set of children of $v$. Let $\\mathcal{M}_{\\mathsf{T}}$ be the set of maximal chains of $\\mathsf{T}$.\nL107: For $C\\in\\mathcal{M}_{\\mathsf{T}}$ and $v\\in C$, let $0pt_{C}(v)$ denote the height of $v$ within $C$; if $v$ is not a leaf, then we let $\\mathrm{ch}_{C}(v)$ be the unique child of $v$ in $C$. For a node $v$ with a descendant $u$, let $(u,v)$ denote the set of nodes (excluding $u$ and $v$) in the unique path from $u$ to $v$. We also write $[u,v)=(u,v)\\cup\\{u\\}$, $(u,v]=(u,v)\\cup\\{v\\}$, and $[u,v]=(u,v)\\cup\\{u,v\\}$.\nL209: ## 3. Maximum Forward Orbit Sizes\nL210: \nL211: In this section, we prove cite41†Theorem 1.1 , which determines the maximum possible size of a forward orbit of the pop-stack operator on an ornamentation lattice. Recall that $\\mathfrak{r}$ denotes the root of the tree $\\mathsf{T}$. The formula for $\\max_{\\delta\\in\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})}|\\text{$\\mathchar 525$}_{\\mathsf{Pop}}(\\delta)|$ stated in cite41†Theorem 1.1 has the following equivalent expressions:\nL253:  | $$\\max_{\\delta\\in\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})}|\\text{$\\mathchar 525$}_{\\mathsf{Pop}}(\\delta)|\\leq\\max_{u\\in\\mathrm{ch}_{\\mathsf{T}}(\\mathfrak{r})}\\mathrm{rk}_{\\mathfrak{r}}(u)+2.$$  |\nL254: \nL255: Combining this with (cite52†4 ) and (cite60†5 ) will then complete the proof of cite41†Theorem 1.1 .\nL298:  | $$|\\Delta_{\\mathsf{T}}(w)|-k\\leq 0pt_{C^{*}}(w)-0pt_{C^{*}}(u),$$  |\nL299: \nL300: and this contradicts (cite65†9 ). ∎\nL301: \nL302: As mentioned above, (cite52†4 ), (cite60†5 ), and cite43†Proposition 3.3 together imply cite41†Theorem 1.1 .\nL380: ###### Proof of cite46†Theorem 1.3 .\nL381: \nL382: Let $A_{n}^{(k)}=|\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n}))|$. Fix integers $n,k\\geq 0$. It follows from cite41†Theorem 1.1 that $A^{(k)}_{n}=1$ if $n\\leq k+1$. (This also follows from [cite36†10 , Theorem 4.13].) Now suppose $n\\geq k+2$; we will show that\nL383: \nL384:  | $$A^{(k)}_{n}=\\sum_{i=k}^{n-1}A^{(k)}_{i}A^{(k)}_{n-1-i}.$$  |\nL385: \nL386: Using this recurrence, it is routine to derive the desired expression for $\\sum_{n\\geq 0}A_{n}^{(k)}x^{n}$.\n"
